.md
# Workers API
WLJS provides asynchronous access to parallel Wolfram kernels. The freeware Wolfram Engine is not limited to a single parallel kernel or subkernel, so you can use this API to build genuinely parallel Wolfram Language workflows.

> Why not `ParallelSubmit`?

The standard Wolfram parallel API is blocking, which makes it awkward to stream data in and out without pausing the main kernel. The Workers API keeps communication event-driven: you attach handlers, send messages, and let the main notebook stay responsive.

Here is a minimal loopback worker:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Workers API
WLJS provides asynchronous access to parallel Wolfram kernels. The freeware Wolfram Engine is not limited to a single parallel kernel or subkernel, so you can use this API to build genuinely parallel Wolfram Language workflows.

> Why not `ParallelSubmit`?

The standard Wolfram parallel API is blocking, which makes it awkward to stream data in and out without pausing the main kernel. The Workers API keeps communication event-driven: you attach handlers, send messages, and let the main notebook stay responsive.

Here is a minimal loopback worker:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
w = WorkerLaunch[Module[{},
  EventHandler[Null, {
    _ -> (EventFire[Null, #] &)
  }]
]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
The worker above sends every message it receives back to the main kernel. Subscribe to messages on the worker object, then send it a value:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
The worker above sends every message it receives back to the main kernel. Subscribe to messages on the worker object, then send it a value:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
EventHandler[w, Function[msg,
  Echo[msg, "worker replied"]
]];
EventFire[w, "Hey!"];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
A worker can also expose small named commands. The main kernel sends an event name plus data; the worker handles the command and fires a result back asynchronously:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
A worker can also expose small named commands. The main kernel sends an event name plus data; the worker handles the command and fires a result back asynchronously:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
commandWorker = WorkerLaunch[Module[{},
  EventHandler[Null, {
    "square" -> Function[x, EventFire[Null, x^2]],
    "total" -> Function[x, EventFire[Null, Total[x]]]
  }]
]];

EventHandler[commandWorker, Function[result,
  Echo[result, "result"]
]];

EventFire[commandWorker, "square", 12];
EventFire[commandWorker, "total", Range[10]];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
You can also start a worker from a `.wl` file. This is useful when the worker setup is longer than a notebook cell, or when the same worker should be reused across notebooks:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
You can also start a worker from a `.wl` file. This is useful when the worker setup is longer than a notebook cell, or when the same worker should be reused across notebooks:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
fileWorker = WorkerLaunch["path to worker.wl"]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Terminate workers when they are no longer needed. Closing them explicitly keeps the notebook from leaving extra Wolfram kernel processes running:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Terminate workers when they are no longer needed. Closing them explicitly keeps the notebook from leaving extra Wolfram kernel processes running:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
If[WorkerReadyQ[w], WorkerClose[w]];
If[WorkerReadyQ[commandWorker], WorkerClose[commandWorker]];
If[WorkerReadyQ[fileWorker], WorkerClose[fileWorker]];

